WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.166 --> 00:00:02.833
(gentle music)

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:14.100
- Innovation is essential

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:17.100
because the society
that we're working with

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:18.180
and trying to protect

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:21.240
from the ills of public health problems

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:23.010
is a society that's changing very quickly,

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:25.260
and when a society changes quickly,

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:27.423
we need to innovate in response to that.

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:31.680
We were very interested
in the question of,

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:33.060
what is causing cancer?

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:35.730
It's a very widespread
concern people have,

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:38.310
but moreover, a given
patient often wants to know

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:40.050
what caused their cancer.

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:42.720
And in the past, what
people have been able to say

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:44.550
are things like, oh, well if you smoke

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:46.170
you are more likely to get cancer.

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:47.850
If you have this genetic preposition,

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:49.680
you are more likely to get cancer.

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:52.020
But that's not really an
answer to the question of,

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:53.790
why did I get cancer?

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:56.700
And on just recently we've been
able to answer that question

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:58.440
by doing tumor sequencing,

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:00.630
being able to see the actual effects

00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:04.020
of what's causing the mutations
that led to that tumor

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:05.580
and then being able to quantify

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:08.130
how those mutations
contribute to the tumor

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:10.290
so that we can actually ask the question,

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:11.670
if someone has lung cancer,

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:14.880
what proportion of your
cancer was caused by smoking?

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:16.620
What proportion was by age?

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:19.080
What proportion was by viral infection?

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:21.450
These sorts of things are
things that in the past

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:23.130
we've never been able to tell someone.

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:25.080
And it's the kind of
innovation that hopefully

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:27.240
will be helpful in improving public health

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:30.300
by being able to tell people
why they're getting ill

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:34.920
and by expressing that
information to those who get ill,

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:37.770
and for having those people
pass that information on

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:40.680
to those they love, so that
they can better understand

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:42.270
what they can do to prevent

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:43.920
the same ills from affecting them.

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:46.800
In the past we've always had to do sort of

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:49.140
what one might call gumshoe epidemiology,

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:52.230
which is like investigating
all the possible causes

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:54.420
that might relate to the people's cancer

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:58.710
and sort of just figuring out
from no information whatsoever

00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:00.960
by being in the area and exploring

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:03.750
what sort of carcinogens
they may have encountered,

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:06.750
why they're more likely to
have come up with a cancer.

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:09.060
But in this case, we could
actually take those individuals

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:12.300
who get cancer and look at the
tumor genome that they have

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:15.960
and better understand what sorts of causes

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:19.410
might be apparent just from
what kinds of mutations occur

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:20.610
in the tumor genome.

00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:23.220
And from that, that
may give us a direction

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:26.849
and a faster route towards
understanding these hotspots

00:02:26.849 --> 00:02:28.800
where individuals get cancer

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:30.660
at higher rates than at other places.

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:32.310
I think the Yale School of Public Health

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:35.250
has a very great appreciation
of the complexity

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:36.930
of the society we live in

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:39.120
and the fact that the
society that we live in

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:40.770
is changing very rapidly.

00:02:40.770 --> 00:02:43.710
And so at the core of its
mission is the idea that

00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:46.200
we need to develop the science

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:49.110
that underlies the policy decision making

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:52.500
and the science progress
that we need to make

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:55.367
in terms of innovating for public health.

00:02:55.367 --> 00:02:58.034
(gentle music)

